{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"## ⚪ §1 — Where LoadBalancer Runs Out\n\nYou already have the argument. Chapter 9 made it, and made it recently.\n\nOne external address per Service"}},"session_id":"7be0df55-8ba5-4cef-93a3-e2b29091adb7","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"ace88eba-6a5f-41e9-b240-ab57d1e5bad9"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":". Fifty Services, fifty addresses, fifty provisioned load balancers, fifty bills, fifty things to manage. And no Service type in that chapter reads HTTP, so a single address cannot serve two paths. A Service kn"}},"session_id":"7be0df55-8ba5-4cef-93a3-e2b29091adb7","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"b0d2dd49-2b2e-4ca9-92d2-45bf1d4e78c4"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ows an address and a port, and stops there *[cross-bearing: see Ch 9 §3 — the Service-type ladder and the exposure ceiling]*.\n\nThat is the ceiling. This section does not re-derive it. It names the vocabulary the"}},"session_id":"7be0df55-8ba5-4cef-93a3-e2b29091adb7","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"48555e3f-e5d6-424a-97f5-3e8405e50fa8"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" rest of the chapter runs on, and gets out of the way.\n\n### The layer boundary\n\nEverything in Chapter 9 operates at what practitioners call **layer 4**. A Service moves packets to an address and a port and has no opinion about what those packets contain. Everything in §2 through §5 "}},"session_id":"7be0df55-8ba5-4cef-93a3-e2b29091adb7","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"a9ba97d2-5b8f-439c-9578-320ea394ac1a"}
